### What happened?

Spreading a non-literal array into a returned array literal produces a schema with a property keyed by the empty string and a `null` element inside `required`:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "": { "type": "array", "items": {} },
    "note": { "type": "string", "const": "flat" }
  },
  "required": [null, "note"]
}
```

`required` must be an array of strings, so this is not valid OpenAPI 3.1 / JSON Schema. A strict validator rejects the whole schema rather than the one field, and codegen produces a property with an empty name.

This looks like the same defect as #1026 and #1068. Both are closed as completed, but it still reproduces on v0.13.42 when the spread is in a plain `return`, outside a `match` expression, and the spread value is a non-literal (here an `array`-cast model attribute).

Where it comes from (v0.13.42): a spread `ArrayItem` has no key node, so `src/Infer/Handler/ArrayItemHandler.php` records `key = null` with `shouldUnpack = true`. `TypeHelper::unpackIfArray` in `src/Support/Type/TypeHelper.php` inlines a spread only when the value is a `KeyedArrayType`, so a plain `ArrayType` survives as a null-key item. `src/Support/Generator/TypeTransformer.php` then pushes that `null` into `$requiredKeys` and casts it to `''` for the property name, and `src/Support/Generator/Types/ObjectType.php` emits both verbatim.

### How to reproduce the bug

`app/Models/Widget.php`

```php
<?php

namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

/**
 * @property array $payload
 */
class Widget extends Model
{
    protected $casts = ['payload' => 'array'];
}
```

`app/Http/Resources/WidgetResource.php`

```php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Resources;

use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Http\Resources\Json\JsonResource;

/** @mixin \App\Models\Widget */
class WidgetResource extends JsonResource
{
    public function toArray(Request $request): array
    {
        return [
            'extra' => [
                ...$this->resource->payload,
                'note' => 'flat',
            ],
        ];
    }
}
```

`routes/api.php`

```php
Route::get('/widgets/{widget}', fn (Widget $widget) => WidgetResource::make($widget));
```

Run `php artisan scramble:export` and read `components.schemas.WidgetResource.properties.extra`.

### Package Version

v0.13.42

### PHP Version

8.5.9

### Laravel Version

13.29.0

### Which operating systems does with happen with?

macOS

### Notes

Reproduced on a fresh `laravel/laravel` install with only `dedoc/scramble` added, no other configuration.

Replacing the spread with `array_merge($this->resource->payload, ['note' => 'flat'])` produces the same shape, so it is not specific to the spread syntax.

Of the issues I hit, this is the only one that emits structurally invalid output rather than an inaccurate type, which is why it seemed worth reporting separately even though the two earlier issues are closed. Emitting `additionalProperties: true` for an unresolvable spread, or simply omitting the null key from `required`, would keep the document valid.

### What happened?

Since 0.13.51, a controller method that calls a method on an object and then passes that object to a method of its form request loses its request documentation. The export reports:

```
ERR [GEN001] Undefined property: Dedoc\Scramble\Support\Type\Reference\PotentialMethodMutatingCallType::$value
Source at vendor/dedoc/scramble/src/Support/Type/Reference/PotentialMethodMutatingCallType.php:36
```

The operation's description then reads "⚠️ Cannot generate request documentation: …", and its parameters are gone, including the path parameters of the route and the query parameters from the form request's `rules()`.

With 0.13.50 the same code is documented correctly. If the two calls are swapped, or if the object's property is read instead of one of its methods being called, 0.13.51 documents it correctly too.

### How to reproduce the bug

```php
// app/Http/Requests/ReproRequest.php
class ReproRequest extends FormRequest
{
    public function rules(): array
    {
        return ['season' => ['sometimes', 'string']];
    }

    public function pick(object $thing): string
    {
        return $this->string('season')->toString();
    }
}

// app/Thing.php
class Thing
{
    public function touch(): void {}
}

// app/Http/Controllers/ReproController.php
class ReproController
{
    // GEN001, no parameters documented
    public function broken(ReproRequest $request, Thing $thing): JsonResponse
    {
        $thing->touch();
        $request->pick($thing);

        return new JsonResponse([]);
    }

    // documented correctly: `season`
    public function fine(ReproRequest $request, Thing $thing): JsonResponse
    {
        $request->pick($thing);
        $thing->touch();

        return new JsonResponse([]);
    }
}

// routes/api.php
Route::get('repro/broken', [ReproController::class, 'broken']);
Route::get('repro/fine', [ReproController::class, 'fine']);
```

Then run `php artisan scramble:export`. `/repro/broken` has no parameters and reports the error above. `/repro/fine` has `season`.

We hit this with a route-bound Eloquent model (a check on the model, then the model passed to the request), but any object will do, as shown above.

### Package Version

0.13.51 (works in 0.13.50)

### PHP Version

8.5.12

### Laravel Version

13.39.0

### Which operating systems does with happen with?

Linux

### Notes

The warning comes from `RequestParametersBuilder::afterAnalyzedNode()` (line 71), which reads the first argument of every method call on the request:

```php
TypeHelper::getArgType($scope, $methodCallNode->args, ['key', 0])->value ?? null
```

After `$thing->touch()`, the type of `$thing` is a `PotentialMethodMutatingCallType`. Since #1309 that class has a `__get()` for its deprecated `callee` and `methodName`, which triggers `E_USER_WARNING` for any other name, but it has no `__isset()`. Without `__isset()`, PHP calls `__get()` for `->value ?? null`, so the `??` no longer guards the read, and the warning ends the documentation of the request. In 0.13.50 the class had no `__get()`, and `->value ?? null` quietly gave `null`.

`->value ?? null` on a type of any kind is used in several places, in `RequestParametersBuilder` alone also for the defaults (lines 129 to 187). So the fix probably belongs in `PotentialMethodMutatingCallType`: an `__isset()` that returns true only for `callee` and `methodName`, as `UnevaluatedValue` pairs its `__get()` with an `__isset()`.

