### What happened?

A resource collection built from `paginate()` loses its pagination envelope when the query chain contains a dynamic where (`whereActive(true)`) or a local scope called through a relation (`$user->posts()->published()`). At runtime the endpoint is paginated, so the spec tells clients `links` and `meta` don't exist.

Replacing the magic call with its explicit form (`where('active', true)`) restores the envelope, and so does calling the scope on a plain builder (`Post::query()->published()`). The paginator itself is inferred fine; it's the unresolved call before `paginate()` that breaks the chain.

Expected:

```json
{ "type": "object", "properties": { "data": {…}, "links": {…}, "meta": {…} }, "required": ["data", "links", "meta"] }
```

Actual:

```json
{ "type": "object", "properties": { "data": { "type": "array", "items": { "$ref": "#/components/schemas/PostResource" } } }, "required": ["data"] }
```

With `JsonResource::withoutWrapping()` the response is documented as a bare array of `PostResource` instead.

This is a regression from 0.13.24, introduced by #1171. 0.13.23 and `ee06630ba` document the envelope; `b422839a5` and every release after it, up to `main` (`0820b49b7`), don't.

### How to reproduce the bug

A fresh `laravel/laravel` 12 app with only `dedoc/scramble` installed, plus:

```php
class Post extends Model
{
    public function scopePublished(Builder $query): void
    {
        $query->whereNotNull('published_at');
    }
}

// added to the default App\Models\User
public function posts(): HasMany
{
    return $this->hasMany(Post::class);
}

/** @mixin Post */
class PostResource extends JsonResource
{
    public function toArray(Request $request): array
    {
        return ['id' => $this->id, 'title' => $this->title];
    }
}

class PostController extends Controller
{
    // Dynamic where: no links/meta
    public function index()
    {
        return PostResource::collection(Post::query()->whereActive(true)->paginate());
    }

    // Local scope through a relation: no links/meta
    public function mine(Request $request)
    {
        return PostResource::collection($request->user()->posts()->published()->paginate());
    }

    // Explicit where: documented with data/links/meta
    public function control()
    {
        return PostResource::collection(Post::query()->where('active', true)->paginate());
    }
}
```

```php
// routes/api.php
Route::get('/posts', [PostController::class, 'index']);
Route::get('/posts/mine', [PostController::class, 'mine']);
Route::get('/posts/control', [PostController::class, 'control']);
```

Then run `php artisan scramble:export`.

### Package Version

0.13.53 and `main` (`0820b49b7`); first broken in 0.13.24

### PHP Version

8.4.27

### Laravel Version

12.70.1 (our app); also reproduced on fresh 12.71.0 and 13.40.0 apps

### Which operating systems does with happen with?

macOS, Linux

### Notes

`EloquentBuilderExtension::getMethodReturnType()` returns `null` for both calls:

- A dynamic `where{Column}` has no method definition and isn't a scope, so nothing types it.
- `forwardRelationCallToRelatedModelBuilder()` only forwards a call when the related model's **builder** class defines the method, so a `scopeX()` on the model isn't found when it's reached through a relation. On a plain builder the scope is resolved via `shouldForwardCallToModel()`.

These calls were unresolved before 0.13.24 too. What #1171 changed is `ReferenceTypeResolver::resolveMethodCallReferenceType()`: a call to a method it can't find now resolves to `NeverType` instead of `UnknownType`. A method call on `never` then resolves to `never` before any extension is asked, so `paginate()` is never seen as a paginator. Changing that one `return new NeverType;` (the "no method definition" branch) back to `UnknownType` on `main` restores the envelope for both endpoints.

#1321 fixes the same chain breaking for `#[Scope]` attribute scopes on a builder; these two triggers aren't covered by it.

We work around it with a `MethodReturnTypeExtension` that returns the instance (builder or relation) for both cases:

```php
public function shouldHandle(ObjectType $type): bool
{
    return $type->isInstanceOf(Builder::class) || $type->isInstanceOf(Relation::class);
}

public function getMethodReturnType(MethodCallEvent $event): ?Type
{
    if ($event->scope->index->getClass($event->instance->name)?->hasMethodDefinition($event->name) === true) {
        return null;
    }
    $model = $event->instance instanceof Generic ? ($event->instance->templateTypes[0] ?? null) : null;
    if (! $model instanceof ObjectType || ! $model->isInstanceOf(Model::class)) {
        return null;
    }
    $isDynamicWhere = Str::startsWith($event->name, ['where', 'orWhere']) && strlen($event->name) > 5;
    $isScope = method_exists($model->name, 'scope'.ucfirst($event->name));

    return $isDynamicWhere || $isScope ? $event->instance : null;
}
```

### What happened?

Hi everyone, I'm facing a strange issue regarding required properties in JsonResources even if they are loaded using the Laravel syntax `$this->when(...)`. I have a resource which wraps a class that has a super generic property that accepts all kinds of items:

 ```php
class MyDto
{
    /**
     * Summary of props
     * @var array<string, mixed>
     */
    public array $props;

    /**
     * Create a new class instance.
     */
    public function __construct(
