### What happened?

When a query-builder wrapper class chains many (~15+) consecutive ->when($cond, fn(): static => $this->someMethod()) calls directly on an Eloquent Builder, where the closure's return type (static, referring to the wrapper class) differs from the type it's chained on (Builder), scramble:analyze / doc generation time grows exponentially with the number of chained calls rather than staying roughly constant or growing linearly/polynomially.

In our real-world case, a single route with 25 such chained calls took over 10 minutes and eventually hit PHP's max_execution_time (600s) in production — this was originally reported as a Datadog error ("Maximum execution time of 600 seconds exceeded") with stack traces pointing into Support/Type/Union.php (Union::wrap()) and Support/Type/TypeWidener.php (TypeWidener::widen()).

We measured wall-clock time for scramble:analyze isolated to a single route, varying only the number of chained when() calls in the filter method:

> chained when() calls/analyze time
> 5/0.66s
> 10/0.88s
> 16/34.75s
> 25 (original)/did not finish in 10+ minutes

Going from 10 → 16 branches (a 60% increase) caused a ~40x slowdown, consistent with exponential rather than polynomial growth, this lines up with Union::wrap()/TypeWidener::widen() doing pairwise merges over a type-set that isn't being collapsed/deduplicated as the chain grows.

