#1260 changed `Infer::analyzeClass()` to re-analyse a class whose indexed definition is a `ShallowClassDefinition`. A raw paginator response reaches `analyzeClass(LengthAwarePaginator::class)` through `ArrayableToSchema` while the index holds a `ShallowClassDefinition` for it. The paginator is then replaced by a full `ClassDefinition`, which later endpoints share through the index.

`AfterLengthAwarePaginatorDefinitionCreatedExtension` runs again on the new definition, but on a full `ClassDefinition`, `lazilyLoadMethodDefinition()` overwrites `$this->methods[$name]` with a fresh, unanalysed definition the first time a method declared on the class is looked up. (`ShallowClassDefinition` overrides it to keep what's there.) So the hand-written `toArray()` is lost and analysed from source.

Either change fixes the repro on `main` (tried separately):

- in `Infer::analyzeClass()`, going back to `if (! $definition)`;
- in `ClassDefinition::lazilyLoadMethodDefinition()`, returning an existing `$this->methods[$name]` instead of replacing it.

I haven't run Scramble's test suite against either.

We work around it with an `AfterClassDefinitionCreatedExtension`, registered before Scramble's own, that calls `$definition->getMethodDefinitionWithoutAnalysis('toArray')` on the paginator classes. That marks `toArray` as loaded before the shallow definition is set.

